/** @mainpage Qore git Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section gitintro Git Module Introduction

    The git module provides git repository operations for Qore through
    <a href="https://libgit2.org/">libgit2</a>. The module supports both
    disk-backed and virtual (in-memory) repositories, enabling git operations
    in environments without persistent filesystem access.

    Key capabilities:
    - dual-mode repository in @ref Qore::Git "Qore::Git" — disk-backed or
      fully virtual with an in-memory working tree
    - virtual filesystem API for reading, writing, and managing files without
      touching disk
    - full branch, tag, diff, log, and status support
    - remote operations (fetch, push) with cooperative cancellation
    - generic @c ConnectionProvider and @c DataProvider infrastructure
    - high-level configuration management with observer pattern
    - GitHub and GitLab PR/MR management via REST APIs

    @section gitnavigation Documentation

    - @subpage gitgettingstarted -- quick-start examples for disk and virtual repos
    - @subpage gitcookbook -- complete code examples for common scenarios
    - @subpage gitreleasenotes -- version history

    @section gitusermodules User Modules

    - <img src="git-logo.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="GitDataProvider app icon"/> @ref gitdataproviderintro "GitDataProvider" -- @c DataProvider API for git
      repository operations; provides read-file, write-file, list-files, commit,
      log, and status actions
    - @ref gitconnectionsintro "GitConnections" -- @c ConnectionProvider helpers
      registering the @c gitlocal and @c gitvirtual connection schemes
    - @ref gitconfigmanagerintro "GitConfigManager" -- high-level configuration
      management with sync/publish semantics and observer-based change detection
      via @c AbstractWatchDataProviderBase
    - @ref gitforgehelperintro "GitForgeHelper" -- GitHub and GitLab pull request
      and merge request management via REST APIs

    @section git_status_constants File status flags

    GitRepository::status() returns a bit mask for each changed path. Flags
    prefixed with INDEX describe staged changes; WT describes the working tree.

    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_INDEX_NEW
      @c GIT_STATUS_INDEX_NEW: New file in the index
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_INDEX_MODIFIED
      @c GIT_STATUS_INDEX_MODIFIED: Modified file in the index
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_INDEX_DELETED
      @c GIT_STATUS_INDEX_DELETED: Deleted file in the index
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_INDEX_RENAMED
      @c GIT_STATUS_INDEX_RENAMED: Renamed file in the index
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_INDEX_TYPECHANGE
      @c GIT_STATUS_INDEX_TYPECHANGE: File type changed in the index
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_WT_NEW
      @c GIT_STATUS_WT_NEW: Untracked file in the working tree
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_WT_MODIFIED
      @c GIT_STATUS_WT_MODIFIED: Modified file in the working tree
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_WT_DELETED
      @c GIT_STATUS_WT_DELETED: Deleted file in the working tree
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_WT_TYPECHANGE
      @c GIT_STATUS_WT_TYPECHANGE: File type changed in the working tree
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_IGNORED
      @c GIT_STATUS_IGNORED: Path matches an ignore rule
    - @anchor GIT_STATUS_CONFLICTED
